\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Interrupted Time Series (ITS) identifies a causal effect by comparing the observed trajectory after an intervention at time t\uc0\u8320  to the counterfactual trajectory that would have continued absent any intervention. The key identification assumption is no confounding events at t\uc0\u8320 ,  no other policy, shock, or structural change occurred simultaneously that could explain any discontinuity. A level change refers to an immediate, one-time jump in the outcome at t\uc0\u8320 , while a trend change refers to a lasting shift in the rate at which the outcome grows or declines after t\u8320 ; both can occur together and are estimated as separate parameters in the regression. One powerful placebo diagnostic is to assign a fake intervention date in the pre-period, a time when the true treatment had not yet occurred, and re-run the ITS model. If this fake date produces large, statistically significant estimates of \uc0\u964 \u8321  or \u964 \u8322 , it is a warning sign that the series was already changing for unrelated reasons, or that the model is over-fitting local variation, undermining the credibility of the real estimate.}